 that those  precedents might be extended to current  circumstances in my view one of the most  interesting features of current Islam  has to do with a debate occasioned by  the claims and practices of al Qaeda and  related groups within the framework of  Sharia reasoning and there's an intense  discussion about those claims in that  framework some of those conversations as  I understand them have to do with what  we might consider in the West a theory  of a just war is that a fair assessment  and how would just war theory compare  with the way in which the Islamic  conversation takes place right I think  there are strong analogies between  between the Western Christian Just War  framework and the Muslim the aspect of  Sharia reasoning that is called the  judgments pertaining to armed struggle  could you explain what those oh yes the  judgments pertaining to armed struggle  these are precedents as I was just  mentioning the the set of sources for  Sharia reasoning these are precedents  consensual precedents set in terms of  opinions issued by recognized religious  scholars now those opinions are always  crafted in response to specific  questions but the general thrust of them  runs very close to the Western Just War  criteria that is to be just a war has to  be authorized by a competent public  authority has to be fought for an  approved cause and with righteous  intention and within the category of  righteous intention  there are among other things the norms  related to the conduct of war that is  that one never directly and  intentionally targets non-combatants and  there is a debate as well about certain  weapons and tactics that is parallel to  the Just War discussion of proportion of  means one of the issues that is of  greatest import in the contemporary  Muslim debate about al-qaeda and related  groups clearly has to do with the  targeting issue can a Muslim fight in a  way that does away with the distinctions  between combatants and non-combatants  and still claim to be involved in a just  war or an honorable war something that  deserves the term jihad as well there is  a debate about whether or not some have  bin Laden and Ayman al-zawahiri and  others actually have the competence to  appeal to Muslims to come and fight in a  particular cause so there are a number  of debate around al Qaeda's appeal to  this judgments pertaining to armed  struggle tradition interesting to see  those parallels like to ask a as  somewhat lime to a question are there  fundamentally different ways of relating  religious faith and the political and  social realms in Christianity and Islam  and if so how would you describe those  differences well one of the great  slogans in Islamic political thought  arising out of populist groups like the  Muslim brothers movement from the 1920s  one of the things that on the sheee  Muslims side the Ayatollah Khomeini  stood for is expressed in the slogan  that Islam knows no distinction between  religion and politics now historically  speaking that's not true Islamic states  have been organized in ways that  recognized spheres of authority some for  political rulers some for religious  rulers it is true that in Muslim  tradition in Sharia reasoning the great  precedent which is cited by contemporary  advic  it's of uniting religion and politics is  that a legitimate state has to have an  Islamic religious establishment and that  is a very strong precedent we have some  people a tradition going back a hundred  years or so who you might call Muslim  advocates of democracy these are very  interesting interpreters of Islamic  tradition some of the most prominent in  Europe and North America they're located  in Europe and North America today and  they argue that this notion of an  Islamic religious establishment is no  longer valid based on their reading of  Sharia sources their engagement in  Sharia reasoning nevertheless there's a  strong tradition here when the Ayatollah  Khomeini was asked one time in an  interview about the gospel tradition  cited by many Christians as a way of  justifying a separation between church  and state he was asked what did Jesus  mean when he said render to Caesar the  things that are Caesar's and to God the  things that are gods and home a nice EDD  I don't think Jesus said that or if he  did he didn't mean what you think he  means because true prophecy are  following the path of prophecy home  Annie said always means to knock down  those idols that would keep human beings  from becoming their true selves and one  of those idols is represented by what he  would have called tyrannical regimes or  more generally regimes that rule by  human law rather than by divine law so  there's a great debate now over exactly  what that means but I think you would  have to say that the drift of Islamic  political thought one of the strong  dimensions over the last hundred years  or so has been this notion that there  needs to be an Islamic religious  establishment and so while you might  distinguish some spheres of religious  and political authority you wouldn't  separate them say along the lines of the  First Amendment to the Constitution of  the United  dates it's interesting that you  mentioned some geographical differences  in Islam between let's say America and  Western Europe and the Middle East Islam  is obviously a very large and complex  religious and cultural tradition can you  cite some other examples in addition to  the one you just noted that where  Islamic traditions of being harnessed to  provide positive political and economic  development will positive political in  economic development may not always of  course be the kind of argument that's  made by Muslim advocates of democracy  we'd have to talk about that some  certainly the most eloquent advocates of  the consonants between Islam and the  International Human Rights regime do  seem right now to be located in Europe  and North America though there is a  great debate interestingly enough in  Iran over these matters once you get the  state committed to the notion that  governance by the Sharia is equivalent  to the rule of law the sources of Sharia  reasoning to allow for considerable  debate about things like the proper role  of women about economic conditions and  the structures of Taxation and so on a  lot of people would also cite a state  like Indonesia as a place where Islam is  interacting with other religious  traditions in a religiously pluralistic  environment and contributing to economic  development especially say a little bit  more about the issue of Islamic  tradition and democracy you mean along  the lines of how the argument is made  yes well if you think of Islamic  tradition as presenting a set of images  that resonate with believers and help  them to think about the kind of  political order they ought to struggle  for the militants appeal say to Muhammad  at Medina where he is fighting against  religious persecution  to establish a safe haven for Islam or  they think about what European and North  American scholars called the early  Islamic conquests the militants present  a vision of this as a a program of  liberation and regime change throwing  off to radical regimes putting in place  good ones so giving a gift of Islamic  government to humanity the Muslim  Democrats by contrast appeal to Muhammad  at Mecca between 610 and 620 to when he  characterized himself as having an order  to preach and to persuade and so even  when his followers appealed to him to  authorize them to use military force  against persecution he said no that's  not the way that I've been instructed  the Muslim Democrats want to appeal to  that image and to say that the fighting  in Medina is very specifically located  it's not a kind of universal symbol or  image for all time they also want to say  that the political regimes put in place  by as a result of the early Islamic  expansion we're not universally just and  we're not universally a benefit to  humankind so its a mix they want to say  that there really is a variety of  precedents in Islamic history for how  you structure the relation of religion  and politics and most of all the Muslim  Democrats appeal to a text in the Quran  there is no compulsion in religion in  chapter 2 verse 256 of the Quran and  they make of that almost a charter if  you will for political thinking in Islam  it becomes the platform on which  religious liberty is the first of human  rights and the rest of the states design  has to flow around that how would their  critics view that passage and what would  they make a very traditional  long-standing precedent in Islam wants  to say that that verse relates  specifically to the question of  conversion to Islam so one who converts  to Islam must do so freely not on  dress as a result of being persuaded by  the message but that doesn't refer they  want to say to some further aspects of  the democratic program for example a  long-standing Islamic tradition if you  convert to Islam but then for some  reason become convinced that it's no  longer for you you become an apostate  and you can be liable to criminal  prosecution and punishment or heresy  blasphemy these kinds of things are  cases in which a long-standing tradition  says it's right for the state to be  involved in delimiting those things as  well if one reads some the tracks of  Zawahri a noose ama bin Laden and  they're not saying anything particularly  new in this regard the argument is that  the imposition of Islamic government is  not covered by that phrase so what  happened in the early program of  liberation and regime change in the  views of the militants is a gift to  humanity and it doesn't run against that  verse you need to have an Islamic  religious establishment they'll say to  point people in the right direction even  if you're not directly trying to bring  about their conversion by compulsion  mm-hmm there have been recent attempts  to find common ground between Christians  and Muslims particularly in the common  word document that was issued by a group  of Muslim scholars what do you think of  such efforts and will they have any  effect in countering Islamic extremism  well the last part of the question of  course is something that is hard to know  at this point my own view is that the  momentum in Islamic populations  particularly among younger people and  particularly if we're thinking outside  of north america and certain circles in  europe the momentum is with the  militants i'm afraid there are lots of  reasons for that something having to do  with  political protest some having to do with  simply catching a vision that presents  to them strong ideal that you might work  for now whether or not the kind of  statements made in a common word really  can reach to such young Muslims and  motivate them to work in another  direction that remains to be seen that's  not to comment of course on the  rightness of the argument or whether or  not it's a good appropriation of Islamic  sources I happen to think it is but we  all know that religious sources are  enormously supple and that making an  argument to convince someone is not  always the same as having a good sound  argument personally I very attracted by  the kinds of arguments made by Muslim  advocates of democracy I think those are  good interpretations of Islamic  religious sources but that's a different  question that which kind of argument has  the momentum particularly among the  young it'll be interesting to watch one  it and this conversation is taking place  within the context of a larger effort  thinking about faith and globalization  right and the term globalization itself  has a term that evokes widely different  responses both here in America and in  Europe and throughout the world can you  tell us what Muslims or different  elements within the Islamic world think  about that term and about the phenomenon  of globalization I'm not entirely sure I  can that is in any 