e vision their  goal really is to establish a state that  is large and powerful enough to affect  the course of things like globalization  either to stop it or to turn it in  another direction  in your writing you've positioned  yourself between those who see a massive  class of civilizations between  Christianity and Islam and those who see  Christianity Islam and Judaism is all  part of the same Western cultural  tradition could you say just one last  word then about why you position  yourself where you do and because that's  what the evidence seems to me to show  religious traditions I think even though  believers present them as they present  themselves as advocates of the faith of  a faith once for all delivered to the  Saints and have this sense that their  beliefs relate to something that is true  in an ultimate an ontological sense of  course that may be true of one or more  of these things but if we look at these  things from the standpoint of what human  beings are doing religious traditions  are living things they develop in line  with the interests needs psychological  purposes of human beings so religion in  the world is actually practiced has  human hands all over it shall we say and  that means in one sense that these  traditions with their frameworks of  authoritative sources like Sharia  reasoning are made for disagreement the  fact that we have disagreement means  that often time the clash is not so much  between traditions but within them or at  least we have to talk rather strongly  about that aspect of it when we're  trying to see how these things match up  in some sort of macro sense want to put  on your prophetic cap for a moment and  tell us what you think is going to  happen in terms of Christian Muslim  relations in the next decade or two  there is going to be continued turmoil  particularly though not exclusively in  the historically Muslim countries one of  the things that is missed when we talk  only about adversarial relationships  between  the United States and European powers  and groups like al-qaeda is that  actually these groups are engaged in a  fight with in traditionally Islamic con  context and that they are doing more  damage more killings more people injured  and so on in those contexts than in  Europe or North America we're going to  see more violence I'm afraid as well  we're going to see continuing efforts by  people on both sides of these great  divides great cultural and religious  divides to try to forge links and to  build bridges so that they can struggle  in their own way against the movement of  violence and extremism I'm afraid we're  in for a rather long struggle that it's  difficult to see exactly when the end  will come John I want to thank you for  giving us some tools for understanding  that struggle and thank you for being  with us today thank you

